--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components bundled in the container image
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The culvert source repository is Apache-2.0. The published container image
additionally installs and distributes the following third-party software
under its own licences:

OpenVPN
  https://openvpn.net/ -- GPL-2.0 with OpenSSL linking exception.
  Installed from the official OpenVPN APT repository.

openvpn-auth-oauth2
  https://github.com/jkroepke/openvpn-auth-oauth2 -- MIT License.
  Copyright (c) Jan-Otto Kroepke. Installed from upstream GitHub releases.

wstunnel
  https://github.com/erebe/wstunnel -- BSD-3-Clause License.
  Copyright (c) Romain Gerard. Installed from upstream GitHub releases.

stunnel
  https://www.stunnel.org/ -- GPL-2.0-or-later with OpenSSL linking
  exception. Installed from Ubuntu APT repositories.

Easy-RSA
  https://github.com/OpenVPN/easy-rsa -- GPL-2.0. Installed from Ubuntu
  APT repositories.

WireGuard tools (wireguard-tools)
  https://www.wireguard.com/ -- GPL-2.0. Installed from Ubuntu APT
  repositories.

Python runtime dependencies
  The image installs a Python dependency tree, pinned by hash. Declared
  directly:

    scalo    https://pypi.org/project/scalo/ -- Apache-2.0.
             Copyright HYPERI PTY LIMITED. Configuration cascade,
             observability surface, secrets providers.
    PyYAML   https://pyyaml.org/ -- MIT License.

  Everything else is transitive. The exact set that ships, with versions and
  SHA256 hashes, is requirements-docker.txt in this repository - an export of
  uv.lock generated at build time, so it is the manifest rather than a copy
  that can drift from one. Licences are those the respective projects publish
  on PyPI.

  Beyond the two above the tree is predominantly Apache-2.0, MIT and BSD. It
  is deliberately not enumerated here: 64 packages restated by hand would be
  stale within a release, and the lockfile cannot be.
